  "findings": [
    "Neumaier compensation-state merge correctly rounded 48/48 measured runs in both runtime modes; scalar Neumaier partial merge correctly rounded 42/48; naive scalar 0/48.",
    "The fresh run reproduced the previous hosted run’s correctness counts on identical deterministic input hashes.",
    "Free-threaded Neumaier-state median speedup versus matching serial Neumaier was 1.2565x; the previous run was 1.2589x. This is a close cross-run match on hosted x86-64 runner classes, not physical-hardware replication.",
    "GIL-enabled Neumaier-state median speedup versus matching serial Neumaier was 0.7601x; the previous run was 0.7576x. The GIL-enabled runs remained slower than the matching serial baseline.",
    "Free-threaded process CPU/wall ratio exceeded 1.05 in 36/48 measured runs; GIL-enabled mode had 0/48. This indicates aggregate CPU concurrency in the tested free-threaded cells, not general application speedup.",
    "Serial math.fsum remained substantially faster than four-worker threaded Neumaier-state on these synthetic fixtures; no general algorithmic speed advantage is claimed."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Two hosted executions per runtime are available for this exact synthetic input/protocol; this is not independent physical-hardware replication.",
    "The GIL-enabled and free-threaded jobs ran on different hosted VM families (AWS versus Azure), so direct cross-runtime timing comparison is confounded.",
    "The reruns use hosted x86-64 runners and deterministic synthetic cancellation inputs only; no representative scientific workload was measured.",
    "Speedup is relative to matching serial Neumaier, not the fastest serial method; serial math.fsum was substantially faster.",
